      "code": "    String s = null;  ",
      "explanation": "A string literal is a series of \r\ncharacters in your code that is \r\nenclosed in double quotes. Whenever \r\nit encounters a string literal in your \r\ncode, the compiler \r\ncreates a String object with its value. \r\nIn this case, the \r\nliteral null is an object reference \r\nvalue that does not \r\nrefer to anything."
    },
    {
      "line_no": 5,
      "code": "    String s1 = \"1\" +\"+\"+ \"2\";    ",
      "explanation": "You can compose long string literals \r\nby using the plus sign to concatenate \r\nstring literals.  Here, s1 is '1+2'"
    },
    {
